  "slides": [
   {
    "kind": "idea",
    "title": "What Domain 5 is about",
    "bullets": [
     "The context window is everything the model can see right now.",
     "It is large but finite, and quality drops as it fills.",
     "Reliability means failing visibly and handing off well.",
     "Domain 5 is about 15 percent of the exam."
    ],
    "note": "Welcome to context management and reliability. The context window is everything the model can see at one moment: instructions, conversation, tool results. It is large but finite, and Anthropic's engineers note that recall gets less accurate as it fills. Reliability is the other half: failures should be visible, uncertainty should be honest, and humans should step in at the right moments."
   },
   {
    "kind": "how",
    "title": "Three forces to manage",
    "bullets": [
     "Loss: summaries and long contexts blur exact facts.",
     "Noise: verbose tool output crowds out what matters.",
     "Silence: hidden errors and missing sources look like certainty.",
     "Each lesson in this domain counters one of these."
    ],
    "note": "Think of three forces working against you. Loss, where summaries and long contexts blur exact details. Noise, where bulky tool output crowds out the important facts. And silence, where a hidden error or a missing source makes an answer look more certain than it is. Every lesson in this domain is a technique for pushing back on one of these."
   },
   {
    "kind": "how",
    "title": "How the six task statements fit",
    "bullets": [
     "5.1 Keep critical facts exact across long conversations.",
     "5.2 Escalate for the right reasons; resolve ambiguity by asking.",
     "5.3 Pass errors up with context in multi-agent systems.",
     "5.4 Explore big codebases without drowning the context.",
     "5.5 Decide where humans review, using calibrated confidence.",
     "5.6 Keep sources and conflicts visible in synthesis."
    ],
    "note": "The six task statements cover the whole journey. Keep facts exact in long conversations. Escalate to people for the right reasons. Pass errors up through multi-agent systems with enough context to recover. Explore large codebases without flooding the context. Decide where human review is really needed. And keep sources and disagreements visible when combining many inputs into one answer."
   },
   {
    "kind": "recap",
    "title": "The mental model to carry",
    "bullets": [
     "Store facts outside the conversation: blocks, files, manifests.",
     "Delegate to subagents to keep the main context clean.",
     "Errors and gaps travel upward with context.",
     "Measure by slice; keep provenance attached."
    ],
    "note": "Carry these ideas into the lessons. Keep important facts outside the conversation, in fact blocks, scratch files and manifests. Use subagents so heavy reading happens in a separate context. Let errors and coverage gaps travel upward with context instead of disappearing. Judge quality slice by slice, and keep every claim attached to its source."
   }
  ],
