  "hook": "A CI reviewer that flags 40 'possible issues' per PR gets ignored, including the one real security bug.",
  "slides": [
   {
    "kind": "idea",
    "title": "Say exactly what counts",
    "bullets": [
     "Vague hedges like 'be conservative' don't change what gets flagged.",
     "Explicit criteria say what to report and what to skip.",
     "Like a referee's rulebook versus 'call fouls sensibly'.",
     "Precision means fewer false alarms, not fewer real findings."
    ],
    "note": "When a review bot is too noisy, the tempting fix is to say be conservative or only flag high confidence issues. That rarely helps, because Claude still does not know what you consider an issue. Explicit criteria work like a referee's rulebook: they spell out exactly what counts as a foul and what does not."
   },
   {
    "kind": "how",
    "title": "Writing explicit criteria",
    "bullets": [
     "List the categories to report, each with a concrete example.",
     "List what to skip: style, linter-enforced checks, generated files.",
     "Define severity levels with examples, not adjectives.",
     "Ask for evidence, such as a file and line, per finding."
    ],
    "code": {
     "lang": "md",
     "text": "## Report\n- Logic errors that change behaviour (e.g. off-by-one in pagination)\n- Queries not scoped to the caller's tenant\n## Do not report\n- Formatting, naming, anything the linter enforces\n- Files under src/gen/\n## Severity\n- high: data loss, security hole, wrong money amount\n- low: misleading comment, unused variable"
    },
    "note": "Good criteria have four parts. What to report, with an example for each category. What to skip, such as formatting or generated code. What each severity level means, illustrated with real cases instead of words like serious. And what evidence each finding must include, which cuts down on guesses dressed up as facts."
   },
   {
    "kind": "how",
    "title": "Trust is earned per category",
    "bullets": [
     "One noisy category makes developers ignore every comment.",
     "Measure false positives per category, not just overall.",
     "Temporarily disable a high-noise category while you fix it.",
     "Keep the accurate categories running in the meantime."
    ],
    "note": "Developers judge a review bot as a whole. If one category, say possible null issues, is wrong most of the time, people stop reading all the comments, including the accurate security ones. So track accuracy per category. Switch off the noisy category while you rewrite its criteria, and keep the trustworthy categories running."
   },
   {
    "kind": "practice",
    "title": "Scenario 5: the null-check flood",
    "bullets": [
     "Bot flags 'possible null' on almost every changed line.",
     "Security findings are accurate, but nobody reads them now.",
     "Disable the null category; keep security and logic on.",
     "Rewrite null criteria with examples of real versus safe cases."
    ],
    "note": "In the continuous integration scenario, the review prompt says to look for potential problems. Most comments are about possible nulls that the type checker already rules out. Developers have learned to scroll past everything. The fix is to pause the noisy category, keep the accurate ones, and write precise criteria with examples before turning it back on."
   },
   {
    "kind": "traps",
    "title": "Exam traps",
    "pairs": [
     {
      "wrong": "Add 'be conservative; only report high-confidence issues'.",
      "right": "Define report and skip criteria with concrete examples.",
      "why": "Vague hedges do not move precision."
     },
     {
      "wrong": "Keep every category on and tell developers to ignore noise.",
      "right": "Disable the high-noise category while fixing it.",
      "why": "Noise erodes trust in the accurate categories."
     },
     {
      "wrong": "Define severity as 'critical means very bad'.",
      "right": "Define each severity level with concrete examples.",
      "why": "Adjectives are read inconsistently; examples are not."
     }
    ],
    "note": "On the exam, any answer that relies on general caution words is usually a distractor. Look for the option that defines categories, exclusions and severities concretely. And if one category is ruining trust, the strong answer pauses that category rather than tolerating the noise or turning off the whole tool."
   },
   {
    "kind": "recap",
    "title": "Remember",
    "bullets": [
     "Explicit criteria beat 'be careful' for precision.",
     "Say exactly what to report and what to skip.",
     "Noisy categories erode trust in accurate ones.",
     "Define severity with concrete examples."
    ],
    "note": "Precision comes from specifics. Tell Claude exactly what counts and what does not, define severity with examples, and watch accuracy per category. When a category is noisy, pause it while you fix it, so the rest of the review stays trusted."
   }
  ],
